{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "printer-status", "title": "Check the printer status"}, {"id": "connection-check", "title": "Separate connection from printing"}, {"id": "controlled-test", "title": "Run one harmless test"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">When a printer stops printing, the quickest safe path is to observe what has changed and narrow the problem. A printer can be powered on but unavailable to the computer, connected but paused, or working in one app while another has selected a different printer. Start with one harmless test and record what you can see.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Start with what the printer and screen show</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use observations rather than repeated retries. You are looking for a small, specific clue.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("Check the printer itself", "Look for power, paper or ink warnings, an offline message and any exact error shown on the printer display.", "#printer-status", "startup", "/img/guides/printer-field-guide-v1.webp", "A household printer with a sheet of paper.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Check the connection path", "Confirm whether the printer uses Wi-Fi or USB, and whether the primary device is connected to the same home network when Wi-Fi is used.", "#connection-check", "applications", "/img/guides/printer-field-guide-v1.webp", "A laptop and connection cable.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Try one controlled print", "Use a short, non-confidential document from one device and note the selected printer and result.", "#controlled-test", "online", "/img/guides/printer-field-guide-v1.webp", "A magnifying glass over a printed test page.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="printer-status"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Check the printer status</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Look at the printer before changing settings. Is it powered on? Does it show an exact message such as offline, paused, paper, ink or cover open? Note the make and model if it is not obvious. An exact message is more useful than “it does not work”.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Check only what is safely visible: paper in the normal tray, a plainly accessible cable and the printer’s own display or lights. Do not disassemble the printer, force a jam, or reset it just to see what happens.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="connection-check"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Separate connection from printing</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Identify the route between the device and printer. For Wi-Fi, check that the device is using the intended home network. For USB, check that the cable is connected at both ends without repeatedly unplugging it. If other household internet services are also failing, the issue may be broader than the printer. See <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/wifi-dropouts-diagnosis/">Wi-Fi dropouts: diagnosis</a> for that separate problem.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Do not widen the fault") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not delete system files, alter router settings or install an unrelated driver while the basic connection is still unclear.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="controlled-test"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Run one harmless test</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">From the device you normally use, confirm that the intended printer is selected. Print one short, non-confidential sample. If it fails, record the exact message and whether the job appears in the print queue. If it works from one app but not another, the app or document may be the next useful comparison.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Windows users can compare the printer’s status and queue through the printer settings. The <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/hardware/printer/troubleshooting-offline-printer-problems-in-windows">Microsoft Windows printer guidance</a> covers status, network checks and the queue. Apple devices can use AirPrint only with a compatible printer on the same network, and not every app supports printing; see <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/109349">Apple AirPrint guidance</a>.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the printer remains unavailable, keep the model, connection type, selected device and exact error together. That gives the next check a clear starting point.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Setting up new equipment instead? Start with <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/set-up-new-home-printer/">setting up a new home printer</a>.</p>
